.SS "static bool Assimp::DefaultLogger::isNullLogger ()\fR [static]\fP"

.PP
Return whether a #NullLogger is currently active\&. 
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
true if the current logger is a #NullLogger\&. Use \fBcreate()\fP or \fBset()\fP to setup a logger that does actually do something else than just rejecting all log messages\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "static \fBvoid\fP Assimp::DefaultLogger::kill ()\fR [static]\fP"

.PP
Kills the current singleton logger and replaces it with a #NullLogger instance\&. 
.SS "static \fBvoid\fP Assimp::DefaultLogger::set (\fBLogger\fP * logger)\fR [static]\fP"

.PP
Setup a custom \fBLogger\fP implementation\&. Use this if the provided DefaultLogger class doesn't fit into your needs\&. If the provided message formatting is OK for you, it's much easier to use \fBcreate()\fP and to attach your own custom output streams to it\&. 
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIlogger\fP Pass NULL to setup a default \fBNullLogger\fP 
.RE
.PP


